{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YGahxw6kWwjhopwbmzUwpG","type":"tool_result","content":"total 8\ndrwx------   3 valentinakoniukhova wheel    96 Oct  3 23:52 .\ndrwxrwxrwt 640 root                wheel 20480 Oct  3 23:52 ..\n-rw-r--r--   1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  4441 Oct  3 23:52 client_brief.md\n# About my business — answers to the questions you would ask me\n\nDon't wait for me, I'm not available this week. Everything I can tell you is\nbelow; where something is missing, make your best assumption and say so.\n\n## Who I am\nI'm Lucía Ferreyra, a potter. My studio is **Taller Lento**, a one-person\nceramics studio in my converted garage in Chacarita, Buenos Aires\n(10 minutes' walk from the Federico Lacroze station). I've been doing it\nfull-time for two years.\n\n## What I sell\n1. **Handmade mugs** (stoneware, my own glazes — the speckled \"yerba\" green is\n   the bestseller). ARS 28,000 each (about US$20). Also small bowls at\n   ARS 22,000 and the occasional set. My kiln and my hands cap me at about\n   60 mugs a month.\n2. **Monthly wheel-throwing classes.** One group: Saturdays 10:00–13:00, four\n   Saturdays a month, max 6 students (I have 6 wheels). ARS 120,000 per person\n   per month (about US$85), clay and firing included. Students keep what they make.\n3. **Taster class** (\"Probá el torno\"): one Saturday afternoon, 2.5 hours,\n   ARS 40,000 (about US$28). I run it when I have enough people asking —\n   maybe once a month.\n\n## Where the money really comes from\nThe mugs are what people see, but the classes are what keep the studio alive.\nA full class group pays more than a whole month of mugs, it's predictable,\nand it doesn't depend on the weather at the fair. Right now the Saturday group\nusually has **3 or 4 of 6 seats filled**. About half of new students stay for\na second month; the ones who stay 3+ months are my best people — they bring\nfriends and buy mugs as gifts. I'd love to open a **second group on Wednesday\nevenings (19:00–22:00)** if I could fill it.\n\nWhen I picture \"it worked\", it's a stranger booking a class — ideally the\nmonthly one, or a taster that turns into the monthly — and then staying.\nA tourist buying one mug is nice but it doesn't change my month.\n\n## How people find me today\n- **Weekend design fair** — Sundays at a craft fair in Palermo (stall costs\n  ARS 35,000 per Sunday). Most mug sales happen here. Maybe 40% of fair buyers\n  are tourists (lots of English speakers, some Brazilians). People pick up the\n  mugs, ask \"do you give classes?\", and I say \"follow me on Instagram\" — and\n  that's mostly where it ends.\n- **Instagram** @taller.lento — 1,900 followers. I post when I remember,\n  maybe once a week: finished pieces, sometimes a Reel of the wheel (those get\n  5–10x more views). Captions in Spanish only.\n- **WhatsApp** — all bookings happen by WhatsApp message. Payment by bank\n  transfer or Mercado Pago link. I keep the student list in my phone contacts.\n- Link in bio goes to a Linktree with my WhatsApp and a PDF price list.\n- No website, no email list, no Google Maps listing.\n- Word of mouth: most current students came from a friend of a student.\n\n## Who buys\n- **Mugs:** tourists at the fair (souvenir, \"made in Buenos Aires\"), and\n  porteñas 25–45 buying gifts.\n- **Classes:** mostly women 28–45 who work in offices or design/tech jobs,\n  living in Chacarita, Colegiales, Villa Crespo, Palermo. They want something\n  off-screen and calm, with their hands. Some come in pairs with a friend.\n  A few expats/digital nomads (English speakers) asked about classes in English\n  — I can teach in English fine.\n\n## Problems I know about\n- Students drop off after the first month, often with \"my schedule changed\".\n- Taster-class people rarely convert to the monthly class — I don't follow up.\n- Fair people ask about classes but I never get their contact.\n- I can't ship internationally (too expensive, breakage), so tourists only buy\n  what they can carry.\n- Sometimes a café asks about wholesale mugs; I don't really want that — the\n  margins are bad and it eats kiln space.\n\n## My limits\n- **Marketing time: about 5 hours a week, total.** The rest is throwing,\n  glazing, firing, teaching and the fair.\n- **Marketing budget: about US$50 a month** (around ARS 70,000), not counting\n  the fair stall.\n- I'm not a marketer. Please write it so I understand it without a\n  dictionary. I'm comfortable with Instagram, WhatsApp and Google Sheets;\n  I don't want to learn complicated tools.\n- My audience is Spanish-speaking locals and English-speaking tourists/expats.\n\n## What I want in 6 months\nSaturday group full (6/6) every month, a Wednesday group running with at\nleast 4 students, and more students staying past their second month.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eaa9753d-c7d3-48e6-b3c6-f635b154a54b","uuid":"e683e203-d9c5-4989-b175-4c48fbee55a7","timestamp":"2026-10-04T02:52:47.124Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 8\ndrwx------   3 valentinakoniukhova wheel    96 Oct  3 23:52 .\ndrwxrwxrwt 640 root                wheel 20480 Oct  3 23:52 ..\n-rw-r--r--   1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  4441 Oct  3 23:52 client_brief.md\n# About my business — answers to the questions you would ask me\n\nDon't wait for me, I'm not available this week. Everything I can tell you is\nbelow; where something is missing, make your best assumption and say so.\n\n## Who I am\nI'm Lucía Ferreyra, a potter. My studio is **Taller Lento**, a one-person\nceramics studio in my converted garage in Chacarita, Buenos Aires\n(10 minutes' walk from the Federico Lacroze station). I've been doing it\nfull-time for two years.\n\n## What I sell\n1. **Handmade mugs** (stoneware, my own glazes — the speckled \"yerba\" green is\n   the bestseller). ARS 28,000 each (about US$20). Also small bowls at\n   ARS 22,000 and the occasional set. My kiln and my hands cap me at about\n   60 mugs